1]Explain indetail about comparable and comparator ?

	->Comparable is used to define the natural ordering of an object,it allows an object to compare itself
	with another object of same type,it is implemented directly by the class you want to sort
	->Comparable belongs to java.lang package
	->Comparable requires  overriding the compareTo() which take 1 arguments
	->Comparable modifies the actual class to make it sortable
	->Comparable provides single,default sorting logic
	
	
	->Comparator is used to define external or custom ordering,it allows to sort the object based on different
	attibutes or criteria without modifying the original class,it is implemented in seperate helper class or as
	a lambda expression
	->Comparator belongs to java.util package
	->Comparator requires overriding the compare() which takes 2 arguments
	->Comparator does not modify the class,making it perfect for sorting the third party classes you cannot edit
	->Comparator allows you create multiple distinct sorting strategies


2]Explain indetail about streams ?

	->In java8, a stream is a sequence of elements supporting sequential and parellel aggregate operations
	it brings functional programming capabilities to java,and the most point to remember is,stream is not a 
	data structure and it does not store any data,instead it takes the data from source like Collection or Array
	it pipes through a series of operations and it delivers the result
	
	Life cycle of a stream:-
	========================
	->A stream pipeline always consists of 3 parts:-
		i)Source : where the data comes from
		ii)Intermediate Operations : These transforms the data like filter() or map(),A key point here is that
		they are lazy,they dont actually execute until a terminal operation is called.
		iii)Teriminal Operations : This triggers the execution and consumes the stream like collect() or
		forEach(),once teriminal operation is invoked,the stream cannot be reused.
